## Network reconnaissance

Before you can secure a network, you need to understand what is on it.

### Identifying your own network configuration

On your **desktop**, ==open a terminal and run:==

```bash
# On: desktop
ip addr show
```

This shows your network interfaces and IP addresses. You should see that your desktop has an interface on the **Internal LAN** segment (10.0.1.0/24). Note your IP address and subnet mask.

==Now check your routing table:==

```bash
# On: desktop
ip route
```

You will likely see something like this:

```
10.0.1.0/24 dev ens19 proto kernel scope link src 10.0.1.10
```

This tells you that your desktop knows how to reach hosts on the 10.0.1.0/24 subnet (the Internal LAN), but notice that there is **no default route**. This means your desktop currently has no idea how to reach any other network segment - including the DMZ (10.0.2.0/24). Any traffic destined for a host outside your local subnet will simply fail.

==Try to ping the web_server in the DMZ to confirm this:==

```bash
# On: desktop
ping -c 2 10.0.2.10
```

This should fail with "Network is unreachable" - your desktop has no route to 10.0.2.0/24.

### Understanding routing and default gateways

To reach other network segments, a host needs a **default gateway** - a router that it sends all non-local traffic to. In your environment, the **gateway** VM is the router between all three segments. For cross-segment communication to work, **every host** that needs to communicate across segments must have a default route configured, pointing at the gateway's IP on its local segment.

The command to add a default route on Linux is:

```bash
sudo ip route add default via \<gateway-ip\>
```

Where \<gateway-ip\> is the gateway's IP address on the **same subnet** as the host you are configuring. For the desktop on the Internal LAN, this is the gateway's 10.0.1.x address. For the web_server on the DMZ, this is the gateway's 10.0.2.x address. For the server on the Server zone, this is the gateway's 10.0.3.x address.

### Understanding IP forwarding

There is another piece of the puzzle. Even if every host has the correct routes set, the gateway itself needs to be configured to actually **forward** packets between its interfaces. By default, a Linux machine will not forward packets that arrive on one interface and are destined for another - it simply drops them.

IP forwarding is controlled by a kernel parameter. You can check whether it is enabled with:

```bash
cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward
```

A value of `0` means forwarding is disabled. A value of `1` means it is enabled. To enable it:

```bash
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
```

Without IP forwarding enabled on the gateway, no traffic will pass between any of the three segments, even if all the routes are correctly configured.

### Reaching hosts on other segments via SSH

Currently, your desktop can only reach hosts on the Internal LAN (10.0.1.0/24). The only other host on this segment is the **gateway**. The **server** (10.0.3.0/24) and **web_server** (10.0.2.0/24) are both on different subnets, so you cannot reach them directly.

However, the **gateway** has interfaces on all three segments. This means that once you SSH into the gateway, you can SSH from there to the server or the web_server - even before IP forwarding or routes are configured. This technique of hopping through an intermediate host is common in network administration.

```bash
# On: desktop - SSH to the gateway
ssh gateway

# On: gateway - SSH to the web_server or server from here
ssh \<web_server-ip\>
ssh \<server-ip\>
```

You will need this technique to complete the first challenge.

### Discovering hosts on the network
§
==Run a ping sweep of the Internal LAN to discover what hosts are on your segment:==

```bash
# On: desktop
nmap -sn 10.0.1.0/24
```

You should find the **gateway** on this segment (the desktop itself will also appear). Note the gateway's Internal LAN IP address - you will need it. The server and web_server are on separate segments and will not appear here until routing is configured.
